---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../styles/about.css";
---

<Base title="About" current="about" description="How to read the windgram, compare models and interpret soaring conditions.">
  <h1>About Windgrams</h1>
  <div class="stack about-content">
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Choose a forecast</h2>
      <p>
        Pick a launch, model and day, then tap an hour to see its wind, lift and sounding.
        Strongest thermals jumps to the hour with the highest forecast thermal strength, w*.
        The chart runs from sunrise to sunset; hatching marks hours with missing forecasts.
        Choose Imperial or Metric in the header to save your preferred units.
      </p>
      <p>Model grids smooth narrow valleys and ridges. Compare the forecast with local terrain, launch exposure and the wind you observe.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Models and coverage</summary>
        <p>
          Choose a country on the launch map. Each launch offers the models that cover it.
          GEPS shows the median and spread of an ensemble, giving a sense of how much its forecasts differ.
          The Soaring page maps HRRR and RRFS over North Sound and Shenandoah–Blue Ridge, and ICON-EU over Armenia.
          New Zealand launches use GFS, GDPS and GEPS point forecasts.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
    <section class="card" id="chart-key">
      <h2>Read the windgram</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Background:</strong> how quickly air cools with height, using <a href="http://wxtofly.net/windgramexplain.html">WxToFly's color scale</a>. Red and orange show rapid cooling; gray shows an inversion.</li>
        <li><strong>Lift w*:</strong> a measure of forecast thermal strength. Your climb rate will vary within each thermal.</li>
        <li><strong>Wind barbs:</strong> wind direction and speed at each height. The staff points toward the wind's source; feathers show speed.</li>
        <li><strong>Paraglider:</strong> estimated lift top for a <span data-unit="lift" data-value="1.143">225 ft/min</span> sink rate. The blue line uses <span data-unit="lift" data-value="1">197 ft/min</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>Outlined cloud:</strong> the height where a rising surface parcel could form cloud. White crosshatching shows humidity above 94%; diagonal hatching shows model cloud cover of at least 30%.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature lines:</strong> connect equal temperatures across the day. Snowflakes mark the lowest freezing level.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Peak lift and highest cloud base can occur at different hours. Compare lift, usable height and wind together.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Heights and soundings</h2>
      <p>
        Chart heights are above mean sea level (MSL). Hover or tap the blue lift line to see its height
        above ground (AGL). This uses launch elevation where available and model terrain elsewhere;
        the readout names the reference. Launch elevation comes from terrain data at the launch coordinates.
        Model terrain is smoother, and some terrain datasets include vegetation and buildings.
      </p>
      <p>
        A model sounding shows temperature, moisture and wind above the selected location and hour.
        A balloon sounding shows measurements from a nearby weather station; its distance and observation
        time appear beside the chart. Morning balloon thermal estimates start at the NWS forecast high
        when it is available, so they describe possible afternoon heating.
      </p>
    </section>
    <details class="card">
      <summary>Forecast updates</summary>
      <p>
        Updates follow each model's schedule. Forecast details show the model run and publication time;
        a notice appears when a forecast is old or an update is delayed. A failed update leaves the last
        complete forecast available. On US launch pages, compare it with the NWS forecast, discussion
        and balloon sounding. Weather station panels show the time of the latest measurement.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="card" id="sources">
